"the-broker|broker|check seat|install seat|ratchet|go-file|receipt" (bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness.mem)

<details>
<summary>15 matches</summary>

```
[grep content: 13 matches across 1 file(s) under /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono]

seat-harness.mem (13 match(es)):
  10: role      = "seat-harness — how the house stands an AI seat: who holds each capability and under whose authority each act runs, the one seat spec across lineages, the lead that runs fresh per step, the broker that lands a walled lead's commits, the spend guard that reads true usage, and the rows still owed"
  33: | ''Lead'' | its worktree, the repo's git dir for reads, its lane root, network | edits, tests, briefs, requests to the broker | commits or pushes itself; writes the main checkout |
  37: ''The trust boundary of the broker, as ruled.'' The broker runs `pnpm install` and git hooks inside a lead's tree, so the broker trusts that tree's code at its own authority. The house holds that boundary as a ruled exception for a single-operator dev tool. A lane whose tree carries code the operator has not trusted runs with a Claude lead instead, or waits for the ratcheted broker (#/owed).
  50: | `lead` (Codex) | its worktree, the repo's git dir, its lane root | on | through the broker alone |
  52: ''Walls that hold by measure.'' The Codex sandbox keeps `.git` read-only inside every granted root, so no sandboxed Codex seat commits. A seat launched inside another seat's sandbox inherits that sandbox and cannot initialize its own runtime state, so the broker launches every seat a lead asks for. A Copilot build seat's tool gate may deny `node` and `pnpm`; its lead or the controller then runs the tests.
  62: * A lead dispatches and waits outside the model: the broker or the controller's watcher blocks on a seat, and the next lead step reads the result path. A lead never polls inside its own context.
  67: <<~ ahu slot=`#/the-broker`>>
  68: !! The broker ~ the hands outside a walled lead
  71: * ''Doors'': `commit` (named relative paths inside the worktree, a message file inside the lane root, `--only`, hooks run); `push` (only when the go file names the branch; never `-u`, never force; the go spends on the attempt); `worktree` (a new path under the lane root, a branch carrying the lane prefix, then submodule init and an offline frozen install); `worktree-remove` (a registered, clean tree, never the broker's own, never `--force`); `launch` (`build`, `read` or `research` alone, never `lead`; allowlisted models; a seat directory inside the lane root with its brief).
  72: * ''The go'' lives outside the lane root and the worktree, at a path the controller names when it starts the broker, so a lead cannot grant its own push.
  73: * ''The broker runs'' from a copy outside the lane and with a launcher outside the lane; a lead's edit to the lane's own copy never reaches the running broker.
  74: * ''A crew cap'' may ride the broker: it refuses a `launch` while a seat of the same kind stays live, naming the cap, so a lead adapts by reading the answer.
  94: # The ratcheted broker: data-only git plumbing with a fixed environment and pinned binaries, a commit bound to an expected old head and an exact tree, hooks and validators in a separate check seat whose receipt the broker verifies, installs in an isolated install seat, a go bound to branch and head, and one outer wall that means the same for Codex and Copilot, proved by planted hooks, filters, a hostile `.npmrc`, a poisoned `PATH` and malicious config.
